itch_live_capture.ndjson — ENC-1028

296 consecutive ITCH 5.0 JSON messages for ONE symbol (BLITZ, stockLocate 28),
captured verbatim off the live hosted feed-simulator at
wss://feed-sim.v3m.xyz/feed on 2026-10-04 at ~12:35:00 UTC. Nothing is
hand-written, re-timed, re-priced or re-ordered: the lines are the bytes the
feed sent, filtered to one stockLocate and kept in wire order.

Why a capture and not a generator: the three atomics ENC-1028 populates are
only meaningful against real values. `timestamp` in particular is the thing
under test — these files carry the feed's actual nanos-since-UTC-midnight
stamps (45284765608047 .. 45300475802429, i.e. 12:34:44 .. 12:35:00 UTC), and
ItchAtomicsTest asserts the stored atomic reduces modulo one day back to
exactly these numbers. A fabricated timestamp could not catch a basis error,
because the fabricator and the asserter would share the mistake.

Message mix: stock_directory 1, add_order 106, add_order_mpid 29,
order_executed 42, trade 42, order_delete 49, order_replace 27.

Two properties of this data that the tests rely on and that are NOT defects in
GMA:
  * 22 order_executed and 18 order_delete messages reference an orderRef added
    before the capture window, so ItchAdapter drops them. That is correct
    mid-stream behaviour, not a parse failure.
  * feed-simulator publishes add_order messages that CROSS the opposite side
    without a matching execution (12 of them in this window), so the
    reconstructed book is sometimes crossed and `spread` is sometimes
    negative. Reproduced by an independent reconstruction outside GMA, so it
    is in the source data. A crossed book being accepted unflagged is already
    pinned by tests/book/CrossedBookTest.cpp (ENC-807 L20). The tests here
    therefore assert `spread == ask - bid` exactly, and never `spread > 0`.
